From: OpenWrt packaging addition
Subject: add fm_stereo_decode and resample_upsample_f as bundled apps

Same addition as utils/csdr's patches/006 - two small, dependency-free
companion tools, added here as new src/apps/ subdirectories (mirroring
nmux's own layout exactly: a two-line CMakeLists.txt calling
add_executable/install) and registered in src/apps/CMakeLists.txt, so
they're built and installed by *this* package - not a separate
OpenWrt package to install.

fm_stereo_decode: FM broadcast stereo (pilot-tone / 38kHz DSB-SC)
decoding. Neither this package nor utils/csdr has it - ha7ilm/csdr
issue #7, "Add WFM stereo decoding" (opened 2016), is still
open/unimplemented in the upstream this was forked from, and csdr2's
own command list (agc/fmdemod/amdemod/dcblock/convert/fft/.../lowpass)
has no equivalent either. PLL-tracks the 19kHz pilot, derives the
38kHz (L-R) reference via the double-angle formula from the locked
NCO, coherently demodulates and lowpass-decimates both (L+R) and
(L-R) with a Hamming-windowed-sinc FIR, de-emphasizes each channel
independently (this package's own deemphasis command is not
stereo-interleave-aware), outputs interleaved float32 L,R. Verified
against a synthetic composite MPX signal with known, distinct L/R
tones: >60dB channel separation at nominal pilot frequency, sustained
under a realistic 8Hz pilot frequency offset from nominal - the same
test with the PLL's loop gains forced to zero collapses to 0dB
separation under that same offset, which is what motivates tracking
the pilot rather than assuming a fixed 19/38kHz.

resample_upsample_f: minimal linear-interpolation upsampler, a
workaround for a memory-safety bug found in this package's own
rational_resampler_ff-equivalent resampling path (same underlying
issue as utils/csdr's rational_resampler_ff - reproducibly reads
uninitialized memory, symptom-sensitive to heap layout/instrumentation,
not root-caused with enough confidence to patch directly). Takes an
optional channel-count argument (default 1/mono) for interleaved
multi-channel data such as fm_stereo_decode's output.

Verified: both build cleanly via native CMake, produce byte-identical/
spectrally-equivalent output to the same tools bundled in utils/csdr
(same source), and cross-compile cleanly for arm-linux-gnueabihf with
real hardening flags (-Wformat -Werror=format-security
-fstack-protector -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=1) and NEON enabled - both come
back tagged Tag_Advanced_SIMD_arch: NEONv1. (Built and linked
independently of libcsdr/fftw3f/libsamplerate, since neither tool
depends on any of them - this sandbox's lack of an ARM-built
fftw3f/libsamplerate, which blocks linking libcsdr.so itself here,
does not affect these two.)

